<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <!--
    The pure tier's structural-predicate LAWS, run on BOTH pipelines (Phase 443 follow-up).

    Deliberately NOT in Fuaran.sln and NOT wired into the FAKE gate — the same posture as
    tests/ids-parity-probe, and for the same reason: it needs a Node runtime, and the default gate
    stays dependency-free. Run it with ./run-pure-tier-laws.ps1.

    It exists because the repo's only Fable leg (the FAKE `Catalog` target) is a COMPILE gate. A compile proves the pure tier TRANSPILES; it makes no claim at all about
    whether the transpiled algebra still obeys its laws, and nothing in a .NET suite can make that
    claim either. `Fuaran.UI.Tests` proves the laws hold on .NET; this proves they hold under Node,
    over the same corpus, and (because the two runs are byte-compared) that both pipelines agree
    about every match SET, not merely that each is internally consistent.

    `Corpus.g.fs` is GENERATED by the runner from `wire-format-fixtures/nodes/` on every run and is
    gitignored. Generating it is what makes the two pipelines provably see identical input: Fable
    cannot read a file, so a probe that let each side load the corpus its own way would be
    comparing two loaders as well as two algebras.

    `Laws.fs` holds the laws themselves and is the SHARED definition site: `Fuaran.UI.Tests` links
    the same file and asserts the same claims through Expecto. It is compiled to JavaScript here, so
    it may use nothing Fable cannot lower — that constraint is stated at the head of the file, and
    it is why the shared site lives on this side of the seam rather than inside the test project.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <Nullable>disable</Nullable>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="Corpus.g.fs" />
    <Compile Include="Laws.fs" />
    <Compile Include="Program.fs" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\Fuaran.UI\Fuaran.UI.fsproj" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="FSharp.Core" />
    <PackageReference Include="Fable.Core" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
